+    "explanation": "Strings look like primitives because we use them constantly, but [[warn:String is actually an object, not a primitive]] — and that distinction explains almost every quirk in this lesson.\n\nSome methods you'll reach for constantly: `.length()` for character count, `.charAt(index)` for one character, `.substring(start, end)` for a slice, `.toUpperCase()`/`.toLowerCase()` for case, `.trim()` to strip leading/trailing spaces, and `.contains(...)` to check for a substring.\n\n[[key:Strings are immutable]] — once created, a String's characters never change. `.toUpperCase()` doesn't modify the original, it hands you back a brand new String; the original is untouched.\n\nAnd here's the trap that catches genuinely everyone at least once: [[warn:never use == to compare String content]]. `==` checks whether two variables point to the exact same object in memory, not whether they contain the same text. Two Strings built separately with `new String(...)` can hold identical text and still fail an `==` check. [[key:Always use .equals() to compare what's actually inside two Strings]] — `.equalsIgnoreCase()` if case shouldn't matter."
