+    "explanation": "[[term:Encapsulation]] is about deciding, on purpose, what parts of your blueprint the outside world is allowed to touch.\n\nJava gives you four levels of [[term:access modifier]]s: `public` (anyone, anywhere), `private` (only inside this exact class), `protected` (this package plus subclasses), and no modifier at all, which defaults to package-level access.\n\n[[key:The standard pattern is: make every field private, and expose controlled access through public getters and setters]]. A getter just hands back the current value — `getName()` returns `name`. A setter is where the real value comes in: it can validate before actually changing anything, like `setAge()` refusing any value outside 0–150 instead of blindly accepting it.\n\n[[warn:Once a field is private, code outside the class genuinely cannot touch it directly]] — not `person.name`, not `person.age = 99`. That's not a limitation, that's the entire point: it forces every change to go through your validation logic instead of letting anything slip in unchecked.\n\nWhy bother with all this? Three real reasons: control over what values are actually allowed in, protection against your object ending up in some invalid state, and the freedom to change how something's stored internally later without breaking anyone else's code that calls your getters and setters."
