-    "explanation": "Before writing Java code, you need three things:\n\n1. JDK (Java Development Kit) — includes the compiler (javac) and runtime (JRE)\n2. An IDE or text editor — IntelliJ, Eclipse, VS Code, or even Notepad!\n3. Understanding the two-step process:\n   • javac compiles .java files into .class bytecode\n   • java runs the bytecode on the JVM\n\nThink of it like cooking: javac is the prep work (chopping ingredients), java is the actual cooking (making the meal). The JVM is the stove that works with any kitchen!\n\nPopular IDEs:\n• IntelliJ IDEA — industry standard, very popular\n• Eclipse — free, open-source, lots of plugins\n• VS Code — lightweight, modern\n\nPro tip: Start with VS Code or IntelliJ Community Edition — they're free and great for beginners!",
+    "explanation": "Alright, before we build anything, let's get your workbench set up.\n\nThere are three things you need before you can write Java with me:\n\nFirst, the [[term:JDK]] — the Java Development Kit. This is your actual toolbox. It bundles the compiler, called [[term:javac]], and the runtime you need to execute what you build.\n\nSecond, somewhere to write the code. Any text editor technically works, but you'll want an [[term:IDE]] — IntelliJ IDEA, Eclipse, or VS Code are the three you'll run into most. [[key:IntelliJ Community Edition and VS Code are both free and won't overwhelm you on day one]] — that's where I'd start if I were you.\n\nThird — and this is the part that trips people up constantly, so pay attention — building a Java program is [[key:always two separate steps, never one]]. Remember that blueprint-and-object idea from last lesson? Same shape here. `javac` takes your `.java` file and turns it into `.class` bytecode — that's you finishing the blueprint. Then `java` hands that blueprint to the JVM, which actually builds and runs the thing. [[warn:Writing the code and running the code are not the same step]] — you always compile first, then run.\n\nOnce you've got a JDK installed, open your terminal and check both `java -version` and `javac -version`. If they both come back with a version number, your workbench is ready. If one's missing, that's the very first thing to fix — nothing else works until it's sorted.",
