-    "explanation": "Type casting is converting one type to another:\n\nWidening (implicit): int → double, char → int\n• Happens automatically\n• Safe — no data loss\n\nNarrowing (explicit): double → int\n• Must use (targetType) cast\n• Can lose precision (3.14159 becomes 3)\n\nLiterals are fixed values written directly in code:\n• 0xFF = 255 in hexadecimal (starts with 0x)\n• 0b1010 = 10 in binary (starts with 0b)\n• 'A' = char literal\n• \"Hello\" = String literal\n\nRemember: String is NOT a primitive — it's a class, but Java treats it specially with literals!"
+    "explanation": "Casting is just moving a value from one box type to another — but which direction you're going in changes how careful you need to be.\n\n[[term:Widening]] (implicit casting) is the easy direction: `int` into `double`, `char` into `int`. Java does this automatically because [[key:you can't lose information going from a smaller type to a bigger one]] — a whole number fits perfectly fine inside a decimal type.\n\n[[term:Narrowing]] is the direction that needs your explicit permission: `double` into `int`. You have to write `(int) pi` yourself, because [[warn:narrowing can genuinely throw information away]] — `3.14159` becomes `3`, and that `.14159` is just gone, not rounded, just chopped off.\n\nWhile we're here — [[term:literals]] are just fixed values written directly into your code. `0xFF` is a [[term:hexadecimal]] literal (255 in plain decimal), `0b1010` is a [[term:binary]] literal (10 in decimal), and `'A'` / `\"Hello\"` are your char and String literals. And one detail worth remembering: [[key:String isn't technically a primitive, it's a class]] — Java just lets you write it with literal syntax because you'd want to use it constantly otherwise."
