     "output": "a + b = 13\na - b = 7\na * b = 30\na / b = 3\na % b = 1\na > b? true\na == b? false\nx && y = false\nx || y = true\n!x = false\nMax: 10",
-    "explanation": "Java has several types of operators:\n\nArithmetic: + - * / %\n• / does integer division (10/3 = 3, not 3.333)\n• % gives remainder (10%3 = 1)\n\nRelational: > < >= <= == !=\n• Compare values, returns boolean\n\nLogical: && (AND), || (OR), ! (NOT)\n• && — both must be true\n• || — at least one must be true\n• ! — flips the value\n\nTernary: condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse\n• Shorthand for if-else\n• (a > b) ? a : b means \"if a > b, use a, otherwise use b\"\n\nPro tip: Use parentheses to make operator order clear!"
+    "explanation": "Operators are how you actually DO something with the values sitting in your variables.\n\n[[term:Arithmetic]] operators — `+ - * / %` — do what you'd expect, with one gotcha: [[warn:dividing two ints always gives you an int back, even if the real answer has a decimal]]. `10 / 3` is `3`, not `3.333`, because Java assumes if both sides are whole numbers, you want a whole number answer. `%`, the [[term:modulo]] operator, gives you the remainder instead — `10 % 3` is `1`.\n\n[[term:Relational]] operators — `> < >= <= == !=` — compare two values and always hand back a `boolean`.\n\n[[term:Logical]] operators combine booleans: `&&` (AND) needs both sides true, `||` (OR) needs at least one side true, and `!` (NOT) just flips whatever it's attached to.\n\nAnd then there's the [[term:ternary operator]] — `condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse` — really just an if-else squeezed onto one line. `(a > b) ? a : b` reads as \"if a is bigger than b, give me a, otherwise give me b.\"\n\n[[key:When operators start mixing together, parentheses aren't optional politeness — they're how you make sure Java evaluates things in the order you actually meant]]."
