     "errorMessage": "OperatorsExample.java:4: error: bad operand types for binary operator '+'\n        System.out.println(\"a / b = \" + a / b);\n                                      ^\n  first type:  String\n  second type: int",
-    "explanation": "Java evaluates left to right with operator precedence!\n\n\"a / b = \" + a / b is evaluated as (\"a / b = \" + a) / b — you can't divide a string by an int!\n\nThe fix: use parentheses to force the division first:\n\"a / b = \" + (a / b)\n\nAlways wrap arithmetic in parentheses when combining with strings!",
+    "explanation": "This is [[term:operator precedence]] biting you. `\"a / b = \" + a / b` doesn't read left-to-right the way you'd hope — `+` and `/` sit at the same precedence level, evaluated left to right, so it actually becomes `(\"a / b = \" + a) / b`. And [[warn:you can't divide a String by an int]] — hence the error.\n\nThe fix is just parentheses: `\"a / b = \" + (a / b)` forces the division to happen first, THEN gets glued onto the string. [[key:Any time you're mixing arithmetic into a string with +, wrap the arithmetic in parentheses]] — it costs nothing and removes all ambiguity.",
