+    "explanation": "Decisions in Java come down to two tools: `if`/`else`, and `switch`.\n\n`if`/`else` chains check conditions top to bottom, and [[warn:order actually matters]] — once one branch's condition is true, Java runs it and skips every branch below, even if a later condition would also technically be true. That's why grade checks always go from highest threshold to lowest: `score >= 90` has to be checked before `score >= 80`, or a 95 would incorrectly land in the B bracket.\n\n`switch` is the cleaner tool when you're checking one variable against several specific values instead of ranges. Each `case` is a possible match, and [[key:break is what stops execution from falling into the next case]] — [[warn:forget a break, and Java just keeps running straight into the next case's code]], which is almost never what you want.\n\nOne clean trick you'll see in switch statements: stacking cases with no code between them, like `case 6: case 7:`, lets both values share the exact same block below."
